% ============================================================================
% government-branch-member — which named officer or institution makes up
% each branch of the U.S. federal government (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% "Which branch is the Supreme Court part of?" is the single most-asked
% elementary civics question, and until this file the stdlib could not
% answer it at all: `civics/` is a BRAND-NEW domain here, the first entry
% against the "Social knowledge → Civics" Major Gap that
% ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md §5.1 has carried unaddressed since the coverage
% baseline was measured. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? government_branch_member(legislative, $M)
%       % congress
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `government_branch_member(branch, member)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the member WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any officer or institution not one of these five
% source-cited members. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a member and
% recall which branch it belongs to, which is the direction an actual
% learner is usually quizzed in:
%
%     ? government_branch_member($B, supreme_court)
%       % judicial
%
% The relation is deliberately MULTI-VALUED on `branch`: the executive
% branch has three named members, so `? government_branch_member(executive,
% $M)` yields three solutions rather than one, the same shape
% `nutrition/food-group-alternative-form.adj` already ships for `dairy`.
% A learner asking "who is in the executive branch?" gets the whole
% enumerated set, each row independently grounded in the same cited
% sentence.
%
% NAMING — why `government_branch_member` and not `government_branch`: the
% predicate names the MEMBERSHIP relation (branch → who is in it), not the
% branches themselves. A future sibling table stating what each branch
% DOES to laws is a different axis and gets its own predicate rather than a
% second column here; a full-tree grep for `legislative`, `judicial`,
% `executive_branch`, `branch_of_government`, `congress`, and
% `supreme_court` across every shipped `.adj` in this stdlib returned
% nothing before this file, so there is no existing table for this content
% to collide with or duplicate.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "Branches of the U.S.
% government" page, which states each branch's membership directly:
%
%     branch (this table's atom label)   member (this table's atom label)   membership quote (verbatim)
%     ---------------------------------  ---------------------------------  ------------------------------------------------------------------
%     legislative                        congress                           "The legislative branch is made up of Congress"
%     judicial                           supreme_court                      "The judicial branch includes the Supreme Court and other federal
%                                                                              courts."
%     executive                          president                          "The president, the vice president, and the president's cabinet are
%     executive                          vice_president                        the members of the executive branch."
%     executive                          president_cabinet                     (one sentence; it names all three executive members at once)
%
% ALL THREE SENTENCES ARE NOW ENCODED, NOT JUST DOCUMENTED HERE. That table
% above is the whole reason this file needed fixing (issue #13928). It named
% three distinct sentences, one per branch — and for a long time only ONE of
% them, the executive sentence, was written into the `table` block as the
% `source`. There were no `cites`. So every answer this library returned was
% evidenced by the executive sentence regardless of which row matched:
%
%     ? government_branch_member(legislative, $M)
%       -> congress, cited by "The president, the vice president, and the
%          president's cabinet are the members of the executive branch."
%
% An answer about the LEGISLATIVE branch, evidenced by a sentence about the
% EXECUTIVE one. That is worse than the known table-level attribution limit
% (issue #13893), where sibling rows at least share a citation that covers
% them; here the citation was about a different subject than the answer.
%
% The lesson is not "someone was careless". The right evidence was identified
% at authoring time and written down HERE, in a comment — and a comment is not
% machine-checkable, so nothing could tell that two thirds of it never reached
% the table. Same root cause as issue #13918: prose that describes provenance
% is not provenance. The two missing sentences are `cites` clauses below.
%
% WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT BUY. Every row's supporting sentence is now
% PRESENT on every answer. It is still not ATTRIBUTED per-answer — the
% executive answers also carry the legislative and judicial sentences as
% corroborations, because provenance in this language is table-level (#13893).
% The gain is the difference between evidence that is incomplete and evidence
% that is wrong, which is the difference worth having.
%
% One detail the table above gets slightly wrong: the page's legislative
% sentence ends in a COLON — "made up of Congress:" — because a list of the
% two chambers follows it. The `cites` clause below carries the colon, which
% also makes it byte-identical to `congress-chamber.adj`'s own `source`.
%
% curl-fetched the raw page directly and read it byte-for-byte before
% writing this file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch) — confirmed all
% three quoted sentences appear verbatim in the page's own "Legislative
% branch" / "Judicial branch" / "Executive branch" sections. `trust
% authoritative` — USA.gov is the U.S. General Services Administration's
% official guide to government information, a first-party .gov publisher
% of exactly the fact being recalled, the same tier this stdlib already
% reserves for NASA/NOAA/USGS/NPS/NIST sources.
%
% ROW-INCLUSION RULE, applied uniformly to all three branches: a row is
% shipped only for a NAMED officer or NAMED institution the page states a
% branch is made up of or includes. Every open-ended CATEGORY phrase the
% same page attaches to a branch is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a member:
%
%   - `other federal courts` (judicial), `special agencies and offices that
%     provide support services to Congress` (legislative), and
%     `Executive departments` / `Independent agencies` / `Other boards,
%     commissions, and committees` (executive) are all open-ended category
%     phrases naming no specific body — there is nothing here to bind a
%     stable atom to.
%   - `senate` and `house_of_representatives` are genuinely named
%     institutions, and the page does name them — but it nests them UNDER
%     Congress in a colon-introduced sub-list ("made up of Congress: The
%     U.S. Senate, The U.S. House of Representatives"), making them
%     CHAMBERS OF CONGRESS rather than direct members of the branch. Their
%     correct home is a separate `congress_chamber` table decoding that
%     same sub-list, not a flattened row here that would silently discard
%     the nesting the source actually states.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table government_branch_member {
    columns branch, member

    row (legislative, congress)
    row (judicial, supreme_court)
    row (executive, president)
    row (executive, vice_president)
    row (executive, president_cabinet)

    source "The president, the vice president, and the president's cabinet are the members of the executive branch."
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
    trust authoritative
    cites "The legislative branch is made up of Congress:" locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
    cites "The judicial branch includes the Supreme Court and other federal courts." locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
}
